145</fpage><lpage>152</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B36"><label>36</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bl&#x000fc;thgen</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Brand</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Cajavec</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Swat</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Herzel</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Beule</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Biological profiling of gene groups utilizing Gene Ontology</article-title><source><italic>Genome Informatics</italic></source><year>2005</year><volume>16</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>106</fpage><lpage>115</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16362912</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B37"><label>37</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Goetz</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Garc&#x000ed;a-G&#x000f3;mez</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Terol</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>High throughput functional annotation and data mining with the Blast2GO suite</article-title><comment>submitted</comment></citation></ref><ref id="B38"><label>38</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Al-Shahrour</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Minguez</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>T&#x000e1;rraga</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>BABELOMICS: a systems biology perspective in the functional annotation of genome-scale experiments</article-title><source><italic>Nucleic Acids Research</italic></source><year>2006</year><volume>34, Web Server issue</volume><fpage>W472</fpage><lpage>W476</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16845052</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B39"><label>39</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Montaner</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>T&#x000e1;rraga</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Huerta-Cepas</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Next station in microarray data analysis: GEPAS</article-title><source><italic>Nucleic Acids Research</italic></source><year>2006</year><volume>34, Web Server issue</volume><fpage>W486</fpage><lpage>W491</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16845056</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Schematic representation of Blast2GO application. GO annotations are generated through a 3-step process: blast, mapping, annotation. InterPro terms are obtained from InterProScan at EBI, converted and merged to GOs. GO annotation can be modulated from Annex, GOSlim web services and manual editing. EC and KEGG annotations are generated from GO. Visual tools include sequence color code, KEGG pathways, and GO graphs with node highlighting and filtering options. Additional annotation data-mining tools include statistical charts and gene set enrichment analysis functions.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJPG2008-619832.001"/></fig><fig id="fig2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Blast2GO annotation rule.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJPG2008-619832.002"/></fig><fig id="fig3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Blast2GO user interface. (A) Main sequence table showing sequence color codes. (B) Graphical tab showing a combined graph with score highlighting. </p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJPG2008-619832.003"/></fig><fig id="fig4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Similarity distribution of Soybean GeneChip. Similarity is computed of each query-hot pair as the sum of similarity values for all matching hsps.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJPG2008-619832.004"/></fig><fig id="fig5" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Species distribution chart of Soybean GeneChip after blastx to NCBI nr.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJPG2008-619832.005"/></fig><fig id="fig6" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Evidence code distribution chart of Soybean GeneChip after mapping to B2G database.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJPG2008-619832.006"/></fig><fig id="fig7" position="float"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Annotation process results for Soybean Affymetrix GeneChip.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJPG2008-619832.007"/></fig><fig id="fig8" position="float"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>GO level distribution chart for Soybean Affymetrix GeneChip. Most sequences have between 3 and 6 GO terms annotated.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJPG2008-619832.008"/></fig><fig id="fig9" position="float"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>InterPro merging statistics. Red column: total number of GO annotations before adding InterPro-based GO terms. Blue: total number of GO annotations after adding InterPro-based GO terms. Green: number of blast-based GO terms confirmed after InterPro merging. Yellow: too general terms removed after InterPro merging.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJPG2008-619832.009"/></fig><fig id="fig10" position="float"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>Molecular function combined graph of GOSlim annotation of the Soybean Affymetrix GeneChip. Nodes are colored by score value.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJPG2008-619832.010"/></fig><fig id="fig11" position="float"><label>Figure 11</label><caption><p>Bar chart for functional category enrichment analysis of Soybean membrane proteins. The <italic>Y</italic>-axis shows significantly enriched GO terms and the <italic>X</italic>-axis give the relative frequency of the term. Red bars correspond to test set (membrane) and blue bars correspond to the whole Soybean genome array.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJPG2008-619832.011"/></fig><fig id="fig12" position="float"><label>Figure 12</label><caption><p>Enriched graph (biological process) of the Soybean membrane subset of sequences.  Node filter has been set at FDR &#x0003c; 1<italic>e</italic>-6. Nodes are colored accordingly to their FDR value in the Fisher exact's test against the whole Soybean genome array.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJPG2008-619832.012"/></fig><table-wrap id="tab1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Blast2GO performance figures of seven cDNAdatasets. FE: percentage of sequences with some functional evidence (Mapping orInterProScan positive). BA: percentage of blast-based annotated sequences, #GO:number of GOs per sequence. GO <italic>L</italic>: mean GO level. IP: percentage of annotationincrease by InterProScan. Ann: percentage of annotation increase by Annex. TA: totalpercentage of annotated sequences (including blast and InterPro). Datasets aredescribed in [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B37">37</xref>].</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">DataSet</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">FE</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BA</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">no. GOs</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">GO <italic>L</italic></th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IP</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Ann</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TA</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>C. clementina</italic></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">70.2 </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">58.2</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.4</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.10 </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7.9</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.8</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">62.3</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>M. incog</italic></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">70.7</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">55.7</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.6 </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.95</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.8 </td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9.9</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">63.9</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>T. harzianum</italic></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">61.1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">47.7</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.6</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.27</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14.4</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">16.2</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">53.4</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>G. max</italic></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">61.8</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">51.1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.3</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.11</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6.1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.8</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">53.5</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>P. flesus</italic></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">50.1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">34.4</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.2</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.07</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">21.9</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10.6</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">45.1</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>A. phagocytophilum</italic></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">56.6</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">42.5</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.91</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">35.4</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20.9</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">49.1</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Whale metagenome</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">69.5</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">50.7</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.45</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">17.6</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">18</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">58.8</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></floats-wrap></article>